<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- The only entitlement the frozen core needs, established by trying
	     without it rather than by assumption.

	     Signed ad hoc under the hardened runtime with no entitlements, the
	     core does not start: dyld refuses the embedded Python.framework because
	     "mapping process and mapped file (non-platform) have different Team
	     IDs" - ad-hoc signatures carry no Team ID at all. With this one
	     entitlement it starts, serves, and every late import loads.

	     allow-jit and allow-unsigned-executable-memory were tried out and are
	     not needed for start-up or for importing the whole stack.

	     Under a single Developer ID team every piece shares a Team ID and
	     library validation passes on its own, so a release could drop this too.
	     It is kept so that a development build and a release load libraries the
	     same way - a difference there would only ever show up after
	     notarisation. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
